SHC - RF part of an RWE / innogy / LIVISI SmartHome Controller (SHC v1)

The central unit of that system contains, next to its AT91SAM9G20 main processor, a
small radio subsystem that is electrically a nanoCUL: an ATmega328P with a CC1100
module (26 MHz reference, 868 MHz). The main processor talks to it over a plain UART.
This device target makes that radio part run culfw.

HARDWARE

  MCU              ATmega328P, 11.0592 MHz crystal
  Transceiver      CC1100 (reports VERSION 0x03), 26 MHz reference
  CC1100 CS        PB2   (ATmega pin 14, pulled up on the board)
  CC1100 GDO0      PD2   (ATmega pin 32)
  CC1100 GDO2      PD3   (ATmega pin 1) -> RX interrupt is INT1, not INT0
  SPI              PB3 / PB4 / PB5 (MOSI / MISO / SCK), shared with the host, see below
  Serial           38400 8N1 on the ATmega UART, wired to the host
  LED              none

TWO THINGS THAT WILL COST YOU AN AFTERNOON IF YOU MISS THEM

1. The SPI bus is shared with the in-circuit programmer.
   The host programs this ATmega over the same MOSI/MISO/SCK lines that carry the
   CC1100 traffic. As long as the host SPI controller owns those pins it holds SCK at
   its idle level, and the ATmega cannot clock the transceiver: every register read
   comes back 0x00, including the status byte, which looks exactly like a dead or
   unpowered chip. After programming, the host must hand the pins back - switch them
   to high-impedance GPIO inputs - before the firmware can use the radio.

2. The core runs at 11.0592 MHz, the clib was written for 8 MHz.
   16 MHz boards divide by two via clock_prescale_set(); 11.0592 MHz cannot be divided
   into 8 MHz, so HAS_16MHZ_CLOCK stays off and F_CPU is 11059200. board.h therefore sets
   CULFW_CORE_HZ, and clib/timebase.h scales the SlowRF time base to it: Timer1 pulse
   measurement and timeouts, my_delay_us(), and the Timer0 tick (125.58 Hz, +0.47 %).
   Without that, Timer0/Timer1 and every SlowRF pulse were a factor of 1.3824 off
   (measured on air on the SHC2, 2026-10-21: FS20 bit periods 0.71x nominal).

BUILDING

	make TARGET=shcCUL868 build
	make TARGET=shcCUL868 bin

The second step produces a raw binary; the ATmega has no bootloader (BOOTRST is
unprogrammed on the factory device) and no USB, so it is flashed in-circuit from the
host rather than by avrdude over a serial port.

FACTORY STATE OF THE ATmega328P

The devices ship with lock bits 0xFC (LB mode 3): the original firmware can be neither
read out nor verified, and a chip erase - which is required before writing anything -
destroys it for good. The fuses survive that erase (low 0xFF, high 0xD9, extended 0xFD),
so crystal operation and brown-out level stay as they were. EESAVE is unprogrammed, so
the erase takes the EEPROM with it.
